  "eps": {
    briefDescription: "Employees' Pension Scheme (EPS-95) gives EPF members with 10+ years of service a monthly pension from age 58 — calculated as (Pensionable Salary × Pensionable Service) ÷ 70 — plus family pension and disability pension.",
    descriptionMd: "The Employees' Pension Scheme 1995 (EPS-95), administered by the Employees' Provident Fund Organisation (EPFO) under the Ministry of Labour & Employment, is a defined-benefit pension for workers in the organised sector. Every employee covered under the Employees' Provident Fund Act, 1952 automatically becomes an EPS member.\n\nEvery month, **8.33% of the employer's 12% EPF contribution** goes into EPS rather than the provident fund. The government also contributes **1.16% of wages** (on the ₹15,000 wage ceiling) to EPS. When a member retires, they receive a monthly pension for life — and after their death, their spouse and children continue to receive family pension.",
    benefitsMd: "| Pension Type | Condition | Rate |\n|-------------|-----------|------|\n| **Superannuation Pension** | Retire at 58 with ≥10 years service | (Pensionable Salary × Pensionable Service) ÷ 70 |\n| **Early Pension** | Age 50–58, ≥10 years service | Superannuation Pension reduced by 4% per year before 58 |\n| **Disability Pension** | Permanent total disablement during service | 50% of Pensionable Salary (min ₹250/month) |\n| **Widow's / Widower's Pension** | On member's death | 50% of member's pension (min ₹1,000/month) |\n| **Children's Pension** | Up to 2 children under 25 | 25% of widow pension per child |\n| **Orphan Pension** | Both parents dead | 75% of widow pension per child (up to 2 children) |\n\n**Pensionable Salary** = average monthly pay drawn during the last 60 months before exit (capped at ₹15,000 unless higher wages were contributed on).\n**Pensionable Service** = actual service years (plus 2 bonus years if service ≥20 years).",
    eligibilityMd: "- Must be a **member of the EPF Scheme 1952** (i.e., employed in an establishment covered under the EPF & MP Act).\n- Monthly wage must not exceed **₹15,000** at the time of joining (those above this can join voluntarily).\n- Must have completed **10 years of contributory membership** in EPS to receive monthly pension.\n- Members with less than 10 years of service can withdraw the **EPS corpus** as a lump sum instead of a pension.",
    exclusionsMd: "- Members who exit EPS with **less than 10 years of service** are not entitled to a monthly pension — they receive a withdrawal benefit (lump sum).\n- Members who withdraw their EPS corpus before completing 10 years lose eligibility for monthly pension.",
    applicationMd: "**To activate your UAN (before retirement):**\n**Step 1:** Visit the EPFO Unified Portal at [unifiedportal-emp.epfindia.gov.in](https://unifiedportal-emp.epfindia.gov.in/) → click 'For Employees' → 'Activate UAN'.\n**Step 2:** Enter your UAN (or Member ID), Aadhaar, name, date of birth and mobile → get OTP → activate.\n\n**To claim EPS pension (on retirement at 58+):**\n**Step 1:** Log in to the EPFO Member Portal with your UAN and password.\n**Step 2:** Go to **'Online Services' → 'Claim (Form-31, 19 & 10C)'**.\n**Step 3:** Verify your bank account and Aadhaar KYC.\n**Step 4:** Choose **'Full EPF Settlement' or 'Pension Withdrawal (Form 10C)'** depending on whether you're claiming pension or lump sum.\n**Step 5:** Submit the online claim; your employer verifies it and EPFO processes the pension or settlement.",
  },

  "nfsmcss": {
    briefDescription: "National Food Security Mission (NFSM) provides subsidised or free inputs — quality seeds, fertilisers, IPM demonstrations, farm machinery — to farmers to increase production of rice, wheat, pulses, coarse cereals, oilseeds and commercial crops.",
    descriptionMd: "The National Food Security Mission (NFSM), launched in 2007–08 by the Department of Agriculture & Farmers' Welfare (Ministry of Agriculture & Farmers' Welfare), aims to increase production of key food and commercial crops through area expansion and productivity enhancement. It does this by taking proven technologies to scale — better seed varieties, integrated nutrient management, water-use efficiency and mechanisation — reaching farmers primarily through cluster demonstrations and direct input support.\n\nNFSM is a centrally sponsored scheme, implemented through the states. Beneficiaries are identified and supported through the Gram Panchayat and the Block Agriculture Office.",
    benefitsMd: "Benefits are provided as **input subsidies** delivered to farmer bank accounts via DBT or as direct distribution through agriculture offices:\n\n- **Cluster Demonstrations (CDs):** Free quality seeds, fertilisers and pesticides for demo plots, helping farmers compare new varieties with their current practice.\n- **Seed Distribution:** Subsidised certified/quality seeds of improved varieties of rice, wheat, pulses, coarse cereals and oilseeds.\n- **Soil Health Management:** Free or subsidised soil-testing and application of micronutrients, gypsum, lime.\n- **Resource Conservation Technology:** Subsidised Zero Till machines, laser land levellers, sprinklers, and other water-saving equipment.\n- **Integrated Pest Management (IPM):** Free pest-scouting and IPM demonstrations.\n- **Training & Capacity Building:** Field Farmer Schools (FFSs) and Kisan Melas.",
    eligibilityMd: "- **All farmers** in NFSM-notified districts are eligible.\n- Priority in demonstration plots and costly inputs is given to:\n  - **SC/ST farmers** (under Special Component Plan and Tribal Sub-Plan, at least 16% and 8% of funds respectively).\n  - Farmers in **low-productivity blocks** identified by the state.\n  - **Small and marginal farmers.**\n- Farmers must have land/lease records in their name (or joint name) in the identified district.",
    exclusionsMd: "",
    applicationMd: "NFSM is not a direct-application scheme for most components. Benefits are **pushed to identified beneficiaries by the Block Agriculture Office** on the basis of the Gram Panchayat's list:\n\n**Step 1:** Contact your **Gram Panchayat or Block Agriculture Officer (BAO)** to register your interest in NFSM demonstrations or seed distribution.\n**Step 2:** The BAO/Gram Sabha identifies beneficiaries for cluster demonstrations, seed subsidy, and machinery in a transparent and time-bound manner.\n**Step 3:** Shortlisted farmers are informed to collect inputs (seeds, fertilisers) at the designated distribution point, or for demonstrations, the BAO coordinates the plot and training.\n**Step 4:** For machinery subsidies, applications may be submitted online through your state's agriculture department portal — check your state agriculture department's NFSM page.",
  },

